                <pb facs="0898" n="802" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg802"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/3970: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dennis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Corinto</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-10-25T10:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">October 25,
                                1926—10 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 6:56
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>178. Referring to Department’s number 95, October 20, 11 a.m.<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d504-f1"/> Admiral Latimer inquires whether use of the term
                    “belligerents” by the Department indicates recognition of belligerency by the
                    United States. I understand there is no recognition of belligerency but
                    admission of insurgency by the United States Government in respect of contending
                    factions on Atlantic coast of Nicaragua. Please instruct. Repeated to
                    Admiral.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dennis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
